## The finding

`crime_00070` — **Duplicate Component Shape**, medium/0.7, `duplicate_component_shape.js`. One `<div>` subtree, hash `e96b55b64336…`, 4 occurrences:

| File | Lines |
|---|---|
| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 142–150 |
| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 152–160 |
| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 101–109 |
| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 112–120 |

I read all four. The subtree is byte-identical except for one text node:

```tsx
<div className="mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4">
  <Link
    href="/dashboard/invoices"
    className="flex h-10 items-center rounded-lg bg-gray-100 px-4 text-sm font-medium text-gray-600 transition-colors hover:bg-gray-200"
  >
    Cancel
  </Link>
  <Button type="submit">Create Invoice</Button>   {/* ← "Edit Invoice" in the edit forms */}
</div>
```

The `mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4` wrapper, the cancel href, and that seven-class button-shaped `<Link>` are repeated verbatim in all four. That last part is the real cost: `Cancel` is a `<Link>` styled to look like a secondary `Button`, so the button styling lives in two unrelated places — `app/ui/button.tsx` and these inline class strings. A designer changing button height or radius fixes `button.tsx` and silently misses four Cancel links.

## Scope caveat, then the proposal

`final-example` and `starter-example` are the finished and starting states of the same tutorial. Duplication *across* them is intentional — consolidating them into one module would break the starter's purpose. So this is two independent 2-file refactors, not one 4-file one. I'd do it in `final-example` and mirror it into `starter-example` only if the starter is meant to already have the component.

**Files that change (per app):**

- **new** `app/ui/invoices/form-actions.tsx`
- `app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` — replace the trailing div; drop the now-unused `Link` import
- `app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` — same

Six files total if you do both apps; three if you do `final-example` only.

## Public API

```tsx
// app/ui/invoices/form-actions.tsx
import Link from 'next/link';
import { Button } from '@/app/ui/button';

export function FormActions({
  submitLabel,
  cancelHref = '/dashboard/invoices',
  cancelLabel = 'Cancel',
  children,
}: {
  /** Text on the submit button — the only thing that actually differs today. */
  submitLabel: string;
  /** Where Cancel navigates. Defaults to the invoices index. */
  cancelHref?: string;
  cancelLabel?: string;
  /** Slot: extra actions rendered left of Cancel (e.g. "Delete invoice"). */
  children?: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <div className="mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4">
      {children}
      <Link
        href={cancelHref}
        className="flex h-10 items-center rounded-lg bg-gray-100 px-4 text-sm font-medium text-gray-600 transition-colors hover:bg-gray-200"
      >
        {cancelLabel}
      </Link>
      <Button type="submit">{submitLabel}</Button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Props:** `submitLabel` (required — the sole real variation), `cancelHref` / `cancelLabel` (defaulted, since both are constant across all four sites today), `children`.

**Slots:** one — `children`, a leading-actions slot before Cancel. That's the extension point a destructive "Delete" action would need, and it's the only slot with a plausible near-term caller. The submit and cancel controls stay closed; if a caller needs to replace them outright it should render its own div rather than grow this API.

**Deliberately not props:** `pending` / `disabled`. Both forms use `useActionState`, so a pending state is foreseeable, but no call site needs it now and guessing the shape (`disabled` vs `pending` vs `aria-disabled`) would bake in the wrong one. Add it when the first caller asks.

Call sites become `<FormActions submitLabel="Create Invoice" />` and `<FormActions submitLabel="Edit Invoice" />`.

## Two adjacent findings this touches

- `crime_00090` / `crime_00091` (**Design Token Escape**, both invoice forms) flag hard-coded `px` values in the same files — the same class of inline-style drift the Cancel link exemplifies. Not fixed by this extraction; worth a follow-up that puts the secondary-button style into `button.tsx` as a `variant="secondary"` and has `FormActions` use it.
- `crime_00071` / `crime_00072` (**Duplicated Role / Status / Plan Check**, `"pending"` and `"paid"`) sit in `edit-form.tsx` and `status.tsx`. Independent of this refactor — flagging it because it's the same "policy repeated by hand" pattern in the same two files.
